<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Ahaziah of Judah</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">d. c. 841 BCE</span> · <a href="/centuries/9th-century-bce">9th century BCE</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Ahaziah was king of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/judah">Judah</a> for about a year around 842–841 BCE, the son of Jehoram and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/athaliah">Athaliah</a>. While visiting his uncle <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jehoram-of-israel">Joram of Israel</a> at Jezreel, he was killed in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jehu">Jehu</a>'s coup, although the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tel-dan">Tel Dan</a> Stele, probably set up by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hazael">Hazael</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/damascus">Damascus</a>, claims these deaths for the Aramean king. His mother Athaliah then seized the throne.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/ahaziah-of-judah.jpg" alt="Ahaziah of Judah" width="320" height="322" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Published by Guillaume Rouille (1518?-1589) · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Ahaziah_of_Judah.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
